% The General Rules for the Interpretation of the Harmonized System, as a
% library for the customs programs of this directory (apparel.le, plastics.le).
% Text: HTSUS Revision 18 (2026), "General Rules of Interpretation",
% https://hts.usitc.gov/ ; the EU Combined Nomenclature has the same six rules.
%
% A domain program supplies, for each heading it models:
%   - "a good is described by heading H" — the terms of the heading, read with
%     the section and chapter notes that define them (GRI 1);
%   - "a good is excluded from heading H" — the notes that exclude;
%   - "heading H1 is more specific than heading H2" — GRI 3(a), each fact
%     with the source that says so;
%   - "a good is within the scope of the model" — which goods it can classify;
%   - its subheadings (GRI 6), usually as a decision table.
% The library then reads:
%   GRI 1   a good is prima facie classifiable in every heading that describes
%           it and does not exclude it;
%   GRI 3   among several, the more specific heading (3(a)); a composite good or
%           a set is classified as consisting of the component that gives
%           it its essential character (3(b)), a judgment recorded with who made
%           it and why; failing both, the heading that occurs last in numerical
%           order (3(c));
%   GRI 6   the subheading, by the same method one level down (domain tables).
% GRI 2(a) (incomplete goods) and 2(b) (mixtures) enter only through 3(b); GRI 4
% (most akin) and 5 (cases, packing) are not modelled.
%
% Headings outside the model. An importer may claim that the good belongs to a
% heading this program does not model (an outerwear jacket of 6101, pajamas of
% 6108, a brassiere of 6212, a case of 4202, a toy of 9503...). Such a claim is
% a scenario fact ("the good is claimed to belong to outerwear"), and the office's
% answer to it is a judgment ("the claim that the good belongs to outerwear is
% rejected, according to CBP, ..., because ..."). A good is classified by the
% model only when every such claim has been rejected; a claim nobody has
% answered yet leaves the classification conditional — a judgment needed.
%
% Provenance. Every rule is labelled and points at its source: a document and
% the passage it encodes ("rule gri_3a with provenance HTSUS General Rules of
% Interpretation, confer "<the words of the rule>":"). The documents say where
% they are published and where their text is (sources/, a copy of the pages
% cited), so the editor shows each rule's passage beside it. Rules that are
% this model's own devices, not the tariff's, have no provenance and a comment.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *a good* is within the scope of the model.
    *a good* is described by heading *a heading*.
    *a good* is excluded from heading *a heading*.
    heading *a heading* is more specific than heading *an other heading*.
    heading *a heading* prevails over heading *an other heading*.
    *a good* is prima facie classifiable in heading *a heading*.
    *a good* is classified in heading *a heading*.
    the heading of *a good* is *a heading*.
    *a good* is not placed by the model.
    *a good* is claimed to belong to *a kind*; undefined.
    the claim that *a good* belongs to *a kind* is *an outcome*; judged.
    *a good* is clear of rival headings.
    *a good* is a composite good; undefined.
    *a good* is a set put up for retail sale; undefined.
    *a component* is a component of *a good*; undefined.
    the essential character of *a good* is given by *a component*; judged.
    *a good* is classified as consisting of *a component*.

the knowledge base gri includes:

HTSUS General Rules of Interpretation is published at "https://hts.usitc.gov/reststop/file?release=currentRelease&filename=General%20Rules%20of%20Interpretation#page=1".
the text of HTSUS General Rules of Interpretation is at "sources/hts/general_rules.txt".

% The question: the heading, for a good the model covers and no rival
% heading claims.
% the model's question: the heading of a good it covers, once every claim to a
% heading it does not model is answered
rule gri_question with provenance HTSUS General Rules of Interpretation,
        confer "Classification of goods in the tariff schedule shall be governed by the following principles":
the heading of a good is a heading
    if the good is within the scope of the model
    and the good is clear of rival headings
    and the good is classified in heading the heading.

% A good the model should classify and cannot: no heading describes it.
% the model does not apply GRI 4: a good that no modelled heading describes is
% reported, not classified
rule gri_unplaced with provenance HTSUS General Rules of Interpretation,
        confer "Goods which cannot be classified in accordance with the above rules shall be classified under the heading appropriate to the goods to which they are most akin":
a good is not placed by the model
    if the good is within the scope of the model
    and the good is clear of rival headings
    and it is not the case that
        the good is classified in heading a heading.

% a heading outside the model is read by the office, not by these rules: every
% claim that the good belongs there must have been rejected
rule gri_rival_headings with provenance HTSUS General Rules of Interpretation,
        confer "classification shall be determined according to the terms of the headings and any relative section or chapter notes":
a good is clear of rival headings
    if for all cases in which
            the good is claimed to belong to a kind
        it is the case that
            the claim that the good belongs to the kind is rejected.

% GRI 1: the terms of the headings and the relative section and chapter notes.
rule gri_1 with provenance HTSUS General Rules of Interpretation,
        confer "for legal purposes, classification shall be determined according to the terms of the headings and any relative section or chapter notes":
a good is prima facie classifiable in heading a heading
    if the good is described by heading the heading
    and it is not the case that
        the good is excluded from heading the heading.

% GRI 3: of two headings that both apply, the more specific one (3(a));
% otherwise, when neither is more specific, the later one (3(c)). GRI 3(b)
% acts on the good itself: see "classified as consisting of" below.
rule gri_3 with provenance HTSUS General Rules of Interpretation,
        confer "When, by application of rule 2(b) or for any other reason, goods are, prima facie, classifiable under two or more headings":
a good is classified in heading a heading
    if the good is prima facie classifiable in heading the heading
    and it is not the case that
        the good is prima facie classifiable in heading an other heading
        and heading the other heading prevails over heading the heading.

rule gri_3a with provenance HTSUS General Rules of Interpretation,
        confer "The heading which provides the most specific description shall be preferred to headings providing a more general description":
heading a heading prevails over heading an other heading
    if heading the heading is more specific than heading the other heading.

rule gri_3c with provenance HTSUS General Rules of Interpretation,
        confer "they shall be classified under the heading which occurs last in numerical order among those which equally merit consideration":
heading a heading prevails over heading an other heading
    if it is not the case that
        heading the heading is more specific than heading the other heading
    and it is not the case that
        heading the other heading is more specific than heading the heading
    and the heading > the other heading.

% GRI 3(b): a composite good, or a set put up for retail sale, is classified
% as if it consisted of the component that gives it its essential character.
% Every other good stands for itself. The domain programs read the material
% and the construction of a good from what it is classified as.
rule gri_3b_composite with provenance HTSUS General Rules of Interpretation,
        confer "shall be classified as if they consisted of the material or component which gives them their essential character":
a good is classified as consisting of a component
    if the good is a composite good
    and the component is a component of the good
    and the essential character of the good is given by the component.

rule gri_3b_set with provenance HTSUS General Rules of Interpretation,
        confer "goods put up in sets for retail sale, which cannot be classified by reference to 3(a)":
a good is classified as consisting of a component
    if the good is a set put up for retail sale
    and the component is a component of the good
    and the essential character of the good is given by the component.

% GRI 3(b) speaks of mixtures, composite goods and sets; any other good is
% classified as what it is
rule gri_3b_other with provenance HTSUS General Rules of Interpretation,
        confer "Mixtures, composite goods consisting of different materials or made up of different components, and goods put up in sets for retail sale":
a good is classified as consisting of the good
    if it is not the case that
        the good is a composite good
    and it is not the case that
        the good is a set put up for retail sale.
